## What's here

| File | Chapter / section | Status |
|---|---|---|
| `1.2.1-sets.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.1 Set Theory | ✅ full transliteration; set algebra + De Morgan (hand proofs), kernel-checked. Exercises → `1.2.1-sets-exercises.md`. |
| `1.2.2-functions.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.2 Cartesian Products and Mappings | ✅ full transliteration; composition associativity/preservation + invertible⇒bijective forward, kernel-checked; bijective⇒invertible marked (needs a choice operator). Exercises → `1.2.2-functions-exercises.md`. |
| `1.2.3-relations.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.3 Equivalence Relations and Partitions | ◑ FOL-tractable part; the three equivalence-projections, kernel-checked. Exercises → `1.2.3-relations-exercises.md`. |
| `1.2.3-partitions.md` | 1 — Preliminaries, §1.2.3 Equivalence Classes and Partitions | ✅ the tractable heart via `classOf(x): Set` — classes nonempty, [x]=[y] iff x∼y, equal-or-disjoint, AND the classes form a partition (via `std/collection.bpa`), kernel-checked, no holes |
| `2.1-induction.md` | 2 — The Integers, §2.1 Mathematical Induction | ✅ induction / strong induction / well-ordering. Exercises → `2.1-induction-exercises.md`. |
| `2.2-division-algorithm.md` | 2 — The Integers, §2.2 The Division Algorithm | ✅ existence + uniqueness inline. Exercises → `2.2-division-algorithm-exercises.md`. |
| `2.3-primes.md` | 2 — The Integers, §2.2 GCD & Primes | ✅ GCD/Bézout + Euclid's Lemma + FTA. Exercises → `2.3-primes-exercises.md`. |
| `3.1-integers-mod-n.md` | 3 — Groups, §3.1 Integers mod n | ✅ the ℤ_n arithmetic proposition (all six parts) as ℤ-congruence. Exercises → `3.1-integers-mod-n-exercises.md`. |
| `3.2-groups.md` | 3 — Groups, §3.2 Definitions and Examples | ✅ full transliteration; definition + 7 examples + basic-properties propositions + Laws of Exponents (`std/group-power.bpa`), verified pure. Exercises → `3.2-groups-exercises.md`. |
| `3.3-subgroups.md` | 3 — Groups, §3.3 Subgroups | ✅ subgroup definition + one-step test + intersection. Exercises → `3.3-subgroups-exercises.md`. |
| `4.1-cyclic-subgroups.md` | 4 — Cyclic Groups, §4.1 Cyclic Subgroups | ✅ full transliteration; all five main-text theorems proved following Judson — ⟨a⟩ is the smallest subgroup, cyclic ⇒ abelian, subgroup-of-cyclic ⇒ cyclic (division algorithm + well-ordering; the elided closure-of-powers step spelled out as `memberPowerInH`), a^k=e ⟺ n\|k, ord(a^k)=n/gcd(k,n). Exercises → `4.1-cyclic-subgroups-exercises.md`. |
| `4.2-complex-multiplicative-group.md` | 4 — Cyclic Groups, §4.2 The Multiplicative Group of ℂ | ◑ ℂ arithmetic + the modulus identities (real proofs via `std/complex-modulus.bpa`); polar form, DeMoivre, roots of unity kept as prose with a note — need trigonometry (`sin`/`cos`/`π`) `bpa` does not have. Exercises → `4.2-complex-multiplicative-group-exercises.md`. |
| `4.3-repeated-squares.md` | 4 — Cyclic Groups, §4.3 The Method of Repeated Squares | ◑ prose-only — an algorithm (binary exponentiation mod n), not a stated theorem; needs a concrete ℤ_n sort + binary representation. Exercises → `4.3-repeated-squares-exercises.md`. |
| `*-exercises.md` | — the segregated chapter exercises | ✅ each Judson end-of-chapter exercise is proved (`std` used liberally), forwarded to a main-text proof, skipped (a brute numeric computation), or **deferred** with a prose note naming the missing machinery. The master deferred index, grouped by machinery, is `EXERCISES-DEFERRED`. |

The set theory rests on `std/set.bpa` (sets over an abstract `Universe` of
elements, fixed membershipwise, with extensionality). Every identity is proved
by the same recipe — extensionality → fix an element → unfold `member(x, ·)` →
one `[using tautology …]` — so every step in the chapter is kernel-checked.

The function theory rests on `std/function.bpa` (maps as opaque `Fn` objects
over a `Universe`, with `apply`/`compose`, an identity, and
injective/surjective/invertible, fixed by their axioms plus **function
extensionality**). Composition associativity is a funcExt rewrite chain; the
preservation and invertibility results are FOL over the defining predicates,
with existential witnesses (`g(b)` for onto, the unpacked inverse for
invertible⇒bijective).

The group theory rests on `std/group.bpa` (the group axioms as a reusable bpa
theory) and uses the `assoc` tactic (associativity-only reordering) for the
non-commutative rearrangements.

## What's deferred (the forcing-function backlog)

Most of the textbook needs machinery bpa does not yet have. Rather than skip
those results silently, each is kept in the relevant `.md` with an in-place note
(`**Deferred:** …` for an exercise, or a `// this section cannot currently be
encoded [reason]` block for a stated theorem) saying exactly what it would
require — a running backlog of standard-library constructions that future
chapters will motivate:

- ~~a concrete **ℤ_n / U(n)** model (modular arithmetic as a group sort)~~ — **the
  ALGEBRA now LANDED** in `std/integer-mod-n.bpa`: ℤ_n as a quotient sort whose
  group/ring axioms lift from ℤ (`ZnGroup`/`ZnGroupPower`/`ZnRing` models, ℤ_n = ⟨1⟩
  cyclic), and U(n) as the units group (`UnitsGroup`). An abstract template modeled at
  a concrete n (e.g. `scratch-needswork/z6.bpa`). What remains for the ℤ_n exercises is
  the FINITE/counting content (next item) — "ℤ_n has exactly n elements", the generator
  COUNT φ(n) — not the algebra,
- **finite sets + cardinality** (counting arguments, Cayley tables, Lagrange,
  Euler's totient φ; "ℤ_n has n elements" / "U(n) is cyclic" as finite facts),
- **matrix / GLₙ** constructions and the reals,
- **sets-of-sets / quotient structure + a relation sort** — Chapter 1's §1.2.3
  (equivalence relations ⟺ partitions) needs equivalence classes `[a]` and the
  partition `X = ⋃[x]`, which `std/set.bpa`'s flat `member : Universe → Set`
  model cannot express; and the **indexed families** `⋃ᵢ Aᵢ` / `⋂ᵢ Aᵢ` need a
  sort of finite sequences of `Set`,
- a **choice / inverse-construction operator** — Chapter 1's §1.2.2 backward
  direction (bijective ⇒ invertible) builds the inverse by "letting `g(b) = a`"
  for the unique preimage `a`; that definite description (turning "for each `b`
  a unique `a`" into a function value) is not expressible in bpa's first-order
  logic, so `invertibleDef`'s `exists g` cannot be discharged,
- a **pair sort `Universe × Universe`** (with a set-of-pairs theory) for the
  §1.2.2 Cartesian products `A × B` and the pairs-based definition of a
  function; `std/function.bpa` sidesteps it by taking `apply(f, x)` primitive.

Each is a self-contained addition; the translation grows as they land.
